  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1012 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nSurface high pressure centered over the Appalachians will \nmaintain its dominance over the Mid-South today and Friday. A\nridge currently centered over eastern Nebraska and Kansas will \namplify a bit over the next 24 hours in response to a deepening \ntrough off the West Coast. The ridge will begin to shift closer to\nthe Mississippi River Valley late today and tonight, becoming \ncentered over the Mid-South by Friday afternoon. This feature will\nreinforce the already dry conditions over the Middle Mississippi \nRiver Valley and boost afternoon highs by a degree or two. \nDewpoints may begin to trend slightly higher Friday afternoon over\neast Arkansas and north Mississippi as southerly flow \nstrengthens, but overall, another clear and dry day is expected. \nNBM continues to favor higher dew points this afternoon and \nFriday. Leaned toward a drier solution resulting in afternoon RH's\nin the 30s to low 40s. The higher dewpoints look more favorable \nin east Arkansas and north Mississippi Friday afternoon while west\nTennessee remains drier. Elevated fire danger will continue \nacross the region today and Friday. Expect high temperatures in \nthe upper 70s to low 80s today, and in the low 80s Friday as the \naforementioned ridge shifts over the area. \n\nWe will begin to notice a pattern change late Friday as the \nsurface high to our east weakens and another develops over \nMichigan. South winds will strengthen over the lower and middle \nMississippi River Valley finally bringing and end to our Fire \nWeather concerns. A ridge will build over the northern Plains \nwhile a southern stream trough shifts out of the Southern Plains. \nThis trough will become negatively tilted early Saturday and shift\nover northern Illinois/Indiana during the afternoon hours. \nDynamic support for thunderstorms looks minimal, with a low \nQPF/steady rainfall event anticipated. Portions of northeast \nMississippi may see over three quarters of an inch Saturday into \nSunday, but most of the region can expect less than one half of an\ninch, lowest in northeast Arkansas. \n\nSouthwesterly flow will gradually turn westerly Sunday. A weak \nridge ahead of a deepening Colorado Low will shift over the \nregion Sunday resulting in lighter and less widespread \nprecipitation and also a decrease in the chance of a thunderstorm.\n\nNext week, the Colorado Low will slowly shift over the Plains \nearly in the week, and approach the middle to lower Mississippi \nRiver Valley Wednesday into Thursday. Timing and exact location of\nthe low is quite uncertain at this time, but a wetter pattern is \nexpected for most of the work week. At this time, NBM favors 1-2 \ninches of rain across most of the Midsouth. Temperatures will \nremain seasonable warm early in the week, and trend only slightly \ncooler after midweek.",
